    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> achieved independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a> on 9 November 1953, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Conference of 1954 secured the withdrawal of French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> forces.</li><li>From 1955 until 1970 Sihanouk's Sangkum was the sole legal party, kept in power by elections marked by fraud and intimidation and by the violent repression of the left.</li><li>Sihanouk proclaimed neutrality, but in 1965 he let North Vietnamese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a> forces build permanent bases on Cambodian soil, and from 1969 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> bombed them.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In March 1955 the king abdicated in favour of his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-suramarit">Norodom Suramarit</a>, and took the title Samdech so that he would have a free hand to engage in politics.</p></aside>
    </section>

