    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> restored its colonial control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> but met local resistance, especially from communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> achieved independence under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a> on 9 November 1953, though the communist United Issarak Front and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> remained active; according to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> P. Chandler, leftist Issarak groups aided by the Viet Minh controlled as much as half the country on the eve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Conference. In April 1954 several Viet Minh battalions crossed into Cambodia, and royalist forces could not force their complete withdrawal.</p><p>The Geneva Conference agreement of 21 July 1954 called for the Viet Minh to withdraw within ninety days and for the Cambodian resistance to demobilise; the French and most of the Viet Minh left in October 1954. Cambodia's representative, Sam Sary, kept the right to seek military aid, so the kingdom accepted only a watered-down neutrality. The agreement also called for elections in 1955 under the International Control Commission. To fight them, Sihanouk abdicated on 2 March 1955 in favour of his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-suramarit">Norodom Suramarit</a>, and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangkum-reastr-niyum">Sangkum Reastr Niyum</a>, the Popular Socialist Community.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sangkum ideology joined Khmer nationalism, loyalty to the monarch, the struggle against injustice and corruption and the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The party taught a conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> reading in which social and economic inequalities were legitimate through the workings of karma, so that the poor might hope, through virtuous and obedient conduct, to be born into a higher station. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> had a Chinese minority of about 300,000, whom Premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> asked in 1956 to stay out of politics and to consider Cambodian citizenship.</p><p>In 1963 Sihanouk nationalised banking, foreign trade and insurance, and in 1964 the National Export-Import Corporation was set up to handle foreign commerce. The declared aims were to give Khmer nationals, rather than Chinese or Vietnamese, a greater role in trade, to remove middlemen and to save foreign exchange. Foreign investment quickly disappeared, and a nepotistic “crony socialism” emerged, with state monopolies parcelled out to Sihanouk's most loyal retainers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> aid, about US$400 million between 1955 and 1963, ended in 1963, and under the deal of 1965 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> bought Cambodian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> at inflated prices.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Royal Khmer Armed Forces (Forces Armées Royales Khmères, FARK) were equipped under a military aid agreement negotiated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1955, and a United States Military Assistance Advisory Group in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> supervised the deliveries. By the early 1960s Washington's aid made up 30% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>'s defence budget. Sihanouk came to see FARK, and the rightist officers led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a>, as Washington's most powerful constituency in the country. Border incursions by South Vietnamese troops pursuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a> insurgents, and overflights by South Vietnamese and United States aircraft, soured relations.</p><p>After the aid ended, the army lacked American equipment and spare parts, poorly replaced by small amounts of Soviet, Chinese and French material. Cambodia was the southern terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-trail">Ho Chi Minh Trail</a>, and after the deal of 1965 North Vietnamese and Viet Cong forces built permanent facilities on its soil, supplied through Cambodian ports. In July 1963 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a> and most of the communist central committee set up an insurgent base in Ratanakiri among the Khmer Loeu, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> insurgency began in 1967–68. From March 1969 the United States bombed the sanctuaries in the Menu airstrikes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the September 1955 election the Sangkum won 83% of the vote and every seat in the National Assembly, a result attributed to fraud and intimidation: votes were cast in full view of Sihanouk's officials, soldiers and police, and in 1957 Sihanouk admitted that thirty-six districts had given majorities to the Pracheachon or the Democrats, where the official results gave them none. In August 1957 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> leaders were publicly humiliated at the Royal Palace and then beaten by police and soldiers. From 1955 until 1970 the Sangkum was the sole legal party.</p><p>The independent left was targeted for destruction. Nop Bophann, editor of the Pracheachon Weekly Paper, was shot dead by state security police in 1959, some 2,000 people were held in a camp in 1960, and a military tribunal with no appeal handed down over 39 death decrees in six months, widely known to be Sihanouk's personal decisions. The leftist Tou Samouth was seized, tortured and murdered in 1962. Sihanouk balanced left against right, bringing leftists such as Hou Yuon, Hu Nim and Khieu Samphan into his governments, and by 1967 he had appointed twenty-three successive Sangkum cabinets.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sihanouk's foreign policy turned on fear of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>'s neighbours, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. After meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-nu">U Nu</a> in 1954 he turned towards nonalignment rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia-treaty-organization">Southeast Asia Treaty Organization</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> in April 1955 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pham-van-dong">Phạm Văn Đồng</a> assured him that their countries would respect Cambodia's independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> became a valuable counterweight to Vietnamese and Thai pressure, and the two states signed a Treaty of Friendship and Nonaggression in 1960. Cambodia severed relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> in August 1963 and broke with Washington in May 1965.</p><p>While Sihanouk talked neutrality in public, by letting the Vietnamese communists base their forces in Cambodia he effectively drew the country into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>. Cambodia was the first foreign government to recognise the Provisional Revolutionary Government of South Vietnam in 1969. The period also formed the later leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a>: a circle of Khmer students in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a>, Ieng Sary and Khieu Samphan, took control of the communist party in the 1960s and went on to establish Democratic Kampuchea.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The repression of 1962 and 1963 drove the communist movement out of the cities and into the countryside. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a> became general secretary of the Workers' Party of Kampuchea in February 1963, and in 1966 the party secretly renamed itself the Kampuchean Communist Party. With the left underground, Sihanouk made concessions to the right. The 1966 election brought an upsurge in the conservative vote, and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a> became prime minister; Sihanouk set up a “counter government” of loyalists and leftists to restrain him, and Lon Nol resigned after a car accident in April 1967.</p><p>The growing North Vietnamese presence in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> destabilised the country politically and economically, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> difficult to deal with. In late 1967 and early 1968 Sihanouk signalled that he would not object to hot pursuit of communist forces into Cambodia, the Menu airstrikes followed in 1969, and full relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> were restored in June 1969. In 1970 a coup headed by Prime Minister Lon Nol abolished the monarchy and established the Khmer Republic, which lasted until the Fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> on 17 April 1975.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> of 1953–1970 was the period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a>'s first rule, from independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to the coup of 1970. His Sangkum held power through rigged elections and the repression of the left while he sought neutrality between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> spread onto Cambodian soil.</em></p>

